1. Select Statement - Used to retrieve the data from table
Syntax - SELECT col_name1,column_name2,--,column_namen FROM table_name;
We can give any number of column name by comma seperated and with any order.Always query execute from backwards.Its start search from table name then with column name. ; denote thats the end of query. We can write capital or small select and from keyword, but best practise is to use capital.
* to retrieve complete table, dont use * until its required, as its send more traffic to database and degrade the performance of the application

2. Select Distinct - Used to retrieve the unique values in the column
Syntax - SELECT DISTINCT(column_name) FROM table_name; - Here we can apply distinct only one column at the time.

3. Count - Used to retrieve total row in the column or the table.
Syntax - SELECT COUNT(column_name) FROM table_name or SELECT COUNT(*) FROM table_name;
Above both return same count because whatever number of column we have in table will have same number of row, so both will return same count
Eg: SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT(column_name)) FROM table_name; - This will find unique count of column name first then it find its count

4. Select Where  - Where is used to do the condition, its used to filter the number of rows returned from the select statement. Here we will use the comparison, logical operator in where
Syntax - SELECT column_name1,---,column_namen from table_name where column_name2 = condition AND column_name3 != condition;
String will be enclosed in ''

5. ORDER BY - Here we can order the resulted row by ASC or DESC.
Syntax - SELECT column_name1, column_name2, column_name3 FROM table_name ORDER BY column_name1 ASC;
Syntax - SELECT column_name1, column_name2, column_name3 FROM table_name ORDER BY column_name1 ASC, column_name3 DESC; - Here it will first order by columnname1, then it again order by column name3. If we did not give ASC or DESC by default it order by ascending order

6. LIMIT - Used to limit the number of row return
Syntax - SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE condition ORDER BY ASC LIMIT 5 - It only return 5 rows
Limit has the lowest priority , it always execute last

7.BETWEEN or NOT BETWEEN- Its get the details between the range given
Syntax - SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE amount BETWEEN 6 AND 7;
Syntax - SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE amount NOT BETWEEN 6 AND 7;
Here between will include 6 and 7 as well when displays the result and not between will exclude that
Date also given in between keyword but needs to follow format 'YYYY-MM-DD', when you given like below
SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE payment_data BETWEEN '2024-10-11' AND '2024-10-15' - It will fetch data till date 14

8. IN or NOT IN - Filter the result which are in IN range
Syntax - SELECT * from table_name WHERE amount IN(5,8,9); - Its only check for amount which are in 5,8 and 9. Not will be reverse of this

9. LIKE and ILIKE  - Used for pattern matching . Like is case sensitivity and ILike is case insensitivity. Here we have two pattern matching
_ and %
% Seq of character - SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE first_name LIKE 'A%' - It will retrieve all the first name which start with A eg Abinaya. '%A% - Any seq of character before and After A eg Tamil ot Thilaga or Diya
- Only one letter -  SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE movie_name LIKE 'Mission Impossible _' it will fetch like below
Mission Impossible 1
Mission Impossible 2
Mission Impossible 3
We can combine and use
SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE first_name ILIKE '_iyA%' it will retrieve Diya
NOT LIKE and NOT ILIKE will be reverse of above